<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patagonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patagonia is the southern end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> river and the southern Andes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a>. It combines mountains, steppe plateaus and a fjord coast, and was long the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehuelche">Tehuelche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> and other peoples. Argentina and Chile occupied it in the late 19th century, Argentina through the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> of 1878 to 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patagonia.jpg" alt="Patagonia" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pat_map.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Patagonia is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> on the west and the Atlantic on the east, and to the south by the Strait of Magellan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beagle-channel">Beagle Channel</a> and the Drake Passage. Its western side, the southern section of the Andes, has lakes, fjords, temperate rainforests and glaciers; the high rainfall there and the low sea-surface temperatures feed the largest ice fields in the Southern Hemisphere outside Antarctica.</p><p>The east has deserts, tablelands and steppes. About 90 percent of the region lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and about 10 percent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Its northern limit is not precisely defined, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and Barrancas rivers are commonly taken as that of Argentine Patagonia. The name comes from patagón, which Magellan used in 1520 for the native peoples whom his expedition thought to be giants, now believed to have been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehuelche">Tehuelche</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation dates back thousands of years. The site of Monte Verde in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> has evidence of human activity around 14,500 years before the present. At the Spanish arrival most peoples were hunter-gatherers, travelling on foot over the eastern plains and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a> and dalcas in the western channels, while communities in a small part of the north-west practised limited agriculture.</p><p>After the failure of Spanish settlement on the Strait of Magellan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé Archipelago</a> became a Spanish base protecting western Patagonia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>, re-established in 1645, acted as a sentry. In the 18th century the Spanish ordered the depopulation of the Guaitecas Archipelago to deprive privateers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of support.</p><p>In the early 19th century many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> migrated to Patagonia and lived as nomads raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> or raiding the Argentine countryside; the stolen cattle travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> along the Camino de los chilenos. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> built a trench, the Zanja de Alsina, in the 1870s against the raids. After independence, Chile and Argentina asserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and colonised the region in the 19th and early 20th centuries; this expansion, with military campaigns, severely disrupted indigenous societies and led to a sharp population decline. Sheep farming, introduced in the late 19th century, became a principal economic activity, and oil was discovered near Comodoro Rivadavia in 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Patagonia is governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Argentina's part is divided into the provinces of Neuquén, Río Negro, Chubut, Santa Cruz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a>. In Chile, the Aysén and Magallanes regions lie entirely within it. Its main economic activities are sheep farming, oil and gas in Argentina, and fishing, salmon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquaculture">aquaculture</a> and tourism in Chile. The steppe is one of the last strongholds of the guanaco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patagonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and then, in the Conquest of the Desert led by General Julio Argentino Roca in 1878–1879 and continued into the mid-1880s, used Remington rifles, the telegraph and railways to overrun the lands of the Mapuche, Ranquel and Tehuelche peoples, killing and displacing thousands and opening <b>Patagonia</b> to settlement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It reached roughly its present shape after the War of the Pacific and the occupation of Araucanía, while the boundary treaty of 1881 with Argentina divided <b>Patagonia</b> and Tierra del Fuego between the two countries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pampas and <b>Patagonia</b> were Mapuche, Ranquel and Puelche country, the Gran Chaco Guaycuru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Genetically the Mapuche differ from the neighbouring peoples of <b>Patagonia</b>, which suggests a different origin or a long separation; mitochondrial DNA points in part to peoples of the Amazon Basin who reached Chile by Andean and lowland routes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune was adapted for the anthems of Cornwall (&quot;Bro Goth agan Tasow&quot;) and Brittany (&quot;Bro Gozh ma Zadoù&quot;), and for &quot;Gwlad Newydd y Cymry&quot; (&quot;The New Country of the Welsh&quot;), written by Lewis Evans, who emigrated to Y Wladfa, the Welsh-speaking settlement in <b>Patagonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1860 he proclaimed himself King Orélie-Antoine I of Araucanía and, days later, added <b>Patagonia</b>, claiming support from Mapuche chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-araucania-and-patagonia">Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia</a></li></ul></section>
